ows nothing. After execution: the fifteen registry names match D1 exactly; explain probed across all token classes (registered shape exits 0 with a shape-reference first line and no Classification; undotted unregistered exits 1 with no Classification; dotted exact and dotted unknown keep todays classification, title and exit); zero implementation symbols appear in the generated document, so the F42 needles still bind the implementation; lint-result schemaVersion untouched; suite 1307 pass 3 skip 0 fail; validate:ci green with the ratchet at 14. DISCRIMINATION PROBE for AC-DRIFT-DETECTABLE, the criterion most at risk of a clean-tree no-op: check mode exits 0 on the committed tree, exits 1 after a grammar inventory gains a member without regeneration, and returns to 0 on restore. That also proves the renderer reads live bindings, since a hardcoded member list would not have moved. Disclosures. (1) THE AUTHORITYS EXECUTE PROMPT WAS WRONG, third instance of the F62 class: it said to expect lint 0/0 once T-001 landed the renderer. Bare lint runs in assertions current mode, which evaluates this changes now-approved BaselineAssertions, so only the D12 graph-anchors window died at T-001 and the nine predicted falsifiers stood until archive. The approved plan (D7, F26) was right and the prompt contradicted it. Caught by the executor, which followed the plan and did not weaken a baseline. (2) THE AUTHORITYS OWN LEDGER CORRECTION WAS WRONG. Earlier this session F21 was corrected to say FIX_ATTEMPT_BUDGET is gone and only FIX_DISTINCT_SIGNATURE_BUDGET remains. Measured now: the constant was renamed to FIX_SIGNATURE_REPEAT_BUDGET (value 2) and gained an exact kind plus key condition, and decideFixBudgetEscalation evaluates trigger R before trigger D. The original grep searched the old name and the distinct name and could not have found the new one. F21s substance still holds, since the raw outcome-blind attempt counter is genuinely gone, but the correction overstated it. Owed to decisions.md after archive; recording it during the open window would have tripped the scope audit. (3) The executor again reported ngrace-execute SKILL.md as stale on the fix budget. It is not. Lines 38, 65 and 90 state both triggers accurately, line 65 explicitly rules out any two fails regardless of signature, and canonical and packaged are byte-identical. This is the second time this exact misreading has occurred on this file, which suggests the leading clause reads as truncatable rather than that the text is wrong. (4) The plan was amended before approval to require the live-reference probe to mutate additively and revert in a finally block. Honored, and strengthened: the test re-validates after the finally to prove the live binding was restored, not merely the size. (5) Banner, preamble sentences, shape heading and JSON envelope shapes were not specified as literal bytes in the plan. The executor bound them in tests and implemented those bytes, and disclosed it. Author judgement inside the plans semantic bounds, not a re-spec. (6) The missing-file drift red used an isolated temporary root, so the assert stays valid now that the committed file exists, and no green file was mutated to manufacture a red. (7) Glob appears twice in the generated document: once as the F60 refusal the plan requires in the preamble, and once as the literal XML tag name Glob inside the observed-write-scope inventory. A tag name in a tag list is not a statement about match semantics, so AC-NO-GLOB-RULE holds. (8) run-ledger.xml is listed in ObservedWriteScope but is written only by fold, which the executor was forbidden to run. Scope is permission, not obligation.</Verdict><Verdict outcome="pass" scope="bundle" classification="plan">Addendum to the bundle verdict, recording an out-of-scope commit made during the open window at the users direction. The F21 re-correction (commit 0b1cb29) edits docs/plans/active/RM-GOVERNED-PATH/decisions.md, which the approved plan D6 deliberately excludes from ObservedWriteScope. The authority had proposed deferring it until after archive to keep the window clean; the user directed no deferrals, which is the correct call on balance because a knowingly wrong ledger entry is worse than a scope note, and the entry claimed a product mechanism was deleted when it had been renamed. Sequenced to limit the cost: the implementation landed first at 6335e3f, the ledger correction went in its own commit afterwards, and no source file moved. BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT THE RE-RUN PROVED. The scope audit reads working-tree porcelain, so committing the ledger edit removed it from the audits view rather than clearing it. The clean re-run (1 changed file, run-ledger.xml, in scope) is therefore evidence that the bundles own tree is scope-clean, not evidence that the ledger edit was in scope. It was not, by design, and this note is the record of that. The audit does not inspect committed history, which is a real limit worth knowing when reading any clean scope result in this roadmap.</Verdict></Verdicts><Decisions><Decision gate="apply" decision="permit"><Requirement id="plan-present" required="true" present="true" blocking="false">status=approved</Requirement><Requirement id="review-verdict" required="true" present="true" blocking="false">outcome=pass</Requirement><Requirement id="no-unresolved-satisfied-ac-clarification" required="true" present="true" blocking="false" /></Decision><Decision gate="archive" decision="permit"><Requirement id="no-open-epoch" required="true" present="true" blocking="false">run/ empty</Requirement></Decision></Decisions></C-SCHEMA-REFERENCE></NgraceRunLedger>
